# Test Implementation Rules

---

## 1. Test Case Naming

Format: `"<subject> - <scenario>: <expected outcome>"`

```c
TEST_CASE("create - NULL handle arg returns INVALID_ARG",          "[module]")
TEST_CASE("send - N receivers, release_cb called exactly once",    "[module]")
TEST_CASE("reentrant - concurrent init same key: all return OK",   "[module]")
```

- Name is self-documenting without opening the body
- Include the triggering condition AND the expected result
- Tag with `[module_name]`; concurrent tests append `[reentrant]`

---

## 2. Test Group Structure

Organize into numbered groups, one group per API or feature cluster.
Mandatory groups (apply all that are relevant to the module's public API):

| Group | Focus |
|---|---|
| 1 | Create / init / destroy — boundary + basic lifecycle |
| 2 | Per-API parameter boundary — NULL, empty, zero, out-of-range, invalid handle |
| 3 | Normal operation — expected behavior with all valid inputs |
| 4 | Asynchronous / deferred delivery — queue or callback delivery, acknowledge |
| 5 | Resource ownership and release — ref-count timing, transfer, NULL release_cb |
| 6 | Remove / unregister — basic removal, wildcard removal, error paths |
| 7 | Post-destroy safety — all APIs reject a destroyed handle gracefully |
| 8 | Capacity / overflow — full buffer, pool exhaustion, drop behavior |
| 9 | Instance isolation — multi-instance non-interference, destroy-one-not-all |
| 10 | Stress — high subscriber/event/instance count, saturation, rapid cycles |
| 11 | Concurrent — all API combinations across simultaneous RTOS tasks |

---

## 3. Boundary and Invalid Argument Coverage

For **every parameter** of **every public API**, write at least one test for each:

- NULL pointer → `ERR_INVALID_ARG`
- Empty string / zero value → `ERR_INVALID_ARG`
- Out-of-range value (e.g. index > capacity) → `ERR_INVALID_ARG`
- Destroyed or unregistered handle → `ERR_NOT_FOUND`
- Mutually exclusive fields both NULL or both set → `ERR_INVALID_ARG`

No API parameter may be left without a boundary test.

---

## 4. Error Code Coverage

Every documented return code must have at least one test that:
1. Triggers the exact condition that produces the code
2. Asserts `TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(EXPECTED_CODE, api_call(...))`

Maintain a comment block at the top of the test file mapping each return code to the
test case(s) that exercise it.

---

## 5. Resource Lifecycle — Every Test Self-Contained

- Each test creates its own resources (handles, queues, semaphores, heap buffers)
- Each test destroys all resources before returning, on every path
- No shared mutable handles between test cases
- Assert allocation success immediately after every `create` / `malloc` / OS primitive call

```c
TEST_CASE("...", "[module]")
{
    module_handle_t h = NULL;
    TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(MODULE_OK, module_create("name", &h));
    /* ... test body ... */
    TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(MODULE_OK, module_destroy(h));  /* always last */
}
```

Cleanup order: free dependent resources (queues, semaphores) before destroying the
parent handle when they are still referenced by that handle.

---

## 6. State Verification — Assert Side Effects, Not Just Return Codes

Every test must verify the **observable effect**, not only the return code:

```c
/* BAD */
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(MODULE_OK, module_send(h, &msg));

/* GOOD */
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(MODULE_OK, module_send(h, &msg));
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL_INT(1, ctx.receive_count);
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL_INT(EXPECTED_ID, ctx.last_id);
```

Use typed assertions (`EQUAL_INT`, `EQUAL_PTR`, `NOT_NULL`, `NULL`, `GREATER_OR_EQUAL`)
— never plain `TEST_ASSERT` for value comparisons.

---

## 7. Ref-counted Resource Release — Exact Timing and Count

When the module uses ref-counted resources with a release callback:

| Scenario | Expected release_count |
|---|---|
| Synchronous delivery only (no deferred consumer) | 1, before send returns |
| 1 deferred consumer, after acknowledge | 1 |
| N deferred consumers, after k acknowledges (k < N) | 0 |
| N deferred consumers, after all N acknowledge | exactly 1 |
| Consumer queue full (dropped) | 1, immediately after send |
| Acknowledge called twice with stale token | still 1 (no double-free) |

Assert `release_count` at every **intermediate** step, not only at the end.

---

## 8. Idempotency and Guard Tests

Verify that repeated or redundant operations are safe and produce consistent results:

- Same-key create called twice → same handle returned, no duplicate resource
- Same-key create called N times sequentially → handle pointer stable, side-effects not multiplied
- Acknowledge / done called twice with the same token → second call safe, callback not re-invoked
- `destroy` called twice → second call returns `ERR_NOT_FOUND`, no crash
- Register then unregister N times in a loop → no leak, no delivery after last unregister

---

## 9. Post-Destroy Safety

After destroying a handle, every API that accepts that handle must return `ERR_NOT_FOUND`
and must not crash or produce phantom side effects:

```c
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(MODULE_OK, module_destroy(h));
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(MODULE_ERR_NOT_FOUND, module_send(h, &msg));
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL_INT(0, ctx.receive_count);  /* no phantom delivery */
```

---

## 10. Instance Isolation

- Operation on instance A must not affect instance B or its registered consumers
- Destroy of instance A must not affect instance B or its state
- Assert both the positive (A affected) and the negative (B unaffected) in the same test

---

## 11. Stress Tests — Required Dimensions

Every module must have stress tests covering all applicable dimensions:

| Dimension | Minimum scale |
|---|---|
| Many consumers, one operation | 30 consumers |
| One consumer, many operations | 200 operations |
| Many instances, each with multiple consumers | 10 instances × 3 consumers |
| Internal pool / capacity saturation | fill to the documented maximum |
| Rapid register / unregister cycle | 50 iterations |
| Rapid create / destroy cycle | 20 iterations |

Verify **exact counts** at the end of each stress test, not just "no crash".

---

## 12. Concurrent Tests — Required Pattern

Use a **two-semaphore barrier** to synchronise all tasks to enter simultaneously:

```c
typedef struct {
    semaphore_t arrive;  /* each task signals arrival */
    semaphore_t go;      /* coordinator releases all at once */
    int         n;       /* number of tasks */
} barrier_t;
```

Required concurrent scenarios (adapt names to the module's API):

| # | Scenario | Pass condition |
|---|---|---|
| C1 | Concurrent create, same key | All tasks return OK; all receive identical handle |
| C2 | Concurrent register from N tasks, then one operation | All N consumers receive the operation |
| C3 | Concurrent operations from N tasks (M each) | Total delivery count = N × M |
| C4 | Producer-consumer race (half register, half operate simultaneously) | No crash, no deadlock |
| C5 | Concurrent deferred producers, drain and acknowledge | `release_count` == items actually received |
| C6 | Sequential idempotent create N times (single task) | Handle stable; side-effects occur exactly once |

Task implementation rules:
- Stack: at least 4× the minimum stack size for the RTOS in use
- Priority: one level above idle
- Every task function must terminate cleanly via the RTOS task deletion API;
  never return from a task function

---

## 13. Test Helper Design

Place all shared helpers above the first test group in a clearly marked block:

```c
/* ── Shared test helpers ──────────────────────────────────── */

typedef struct {
    int receive_count;   /* incremented on each delivery */
    int last_id;         /* last received event/message id */
    int release_count;   /* incremented in release callback */
} test_ctx_t;

static void count_cb(const event_t *e, void *ctx) { ... }
static void release_cb(const void *data, void *ctx) { ... }
static data_t *make_data(int val) { ... }  /* returns NULL-checkable pointer */
```

Rules:
- Context structs use plain `int` counters, zero-initialised with `= {0}`
- Release callbacks are responsible for freeing the data they receive
- Data factory helpers must return a `NULL`-checkable pointer; test must assert non-NULL

---

## 14. What Must NOT Be in Tests

- No shared global mutable state between test cases
- No timed sleep as a substitute for synchronisation — use semaphores or condition variables
- No unchecked allocation — assert non-NULL immediately after every `create` / `malloc`
- No test that asserts only a return code without verifying the observable side effect
- No test that leaks any resource (queue, semaphore, heap buffer, handle) on any code path

---

## 15. Test Report

Write one `TEST_REPORT.md` per test binary, placed in the same directory as the test source.
Update the report every time the test suite is re-run.
This mandatory report is an explicit exception to the minimal-doc constraint in `docs-output.mdc`.

### Required sections

**Header block** — one field per line, no prose:

```
Date:      YYYY-MM-DD
Platform:  <OS name + version> (<arch>)
Compiler:  <name + version>
RTOS:      <name + version> (<port>)
Framework: <test framework name + version>
Source:    <relative path to test file>
```

**Overall summary** — single table:

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Total | N |
| Pass | N |
| Fail | N |
| Skip | N |
| Result | **PASS** / **FAIL** |

**Per-group detail** — one sub-section per group, heading format:
`### Group N — <feature> (<count> cases)`

Each sub-section contains:
- One sentence stating what the group validates (coverage angle, not test names)
- A result table:

| # | Test case name | Result |
|---|---|---|

For stress groups, add a `Parameters` column with the scale values used.
For concurrent groups, add a `Concurrency config` column (e.g. `8 tasks × 20 ops`).

Test case names in the table must match the `TEST_CASE(...)` string exactly.

**Code path coverage** — two tables:

*Covered:*

| Code path / branch | Covered by test # |
|---|---|

Map every public-API branch (NULL guard, error return, fast path, slow path,
ref-count decrement, release trigger, drop/overflow) to the test number(s) that reach it.

*Uncovered:*

| Path | Reason | Injection needed |
|---|---|---|

List only paths that require fault injection (allocation failure, hardware error).
This table must always be present; write `None` if all paths are covered.

**Log summary** — list only log lines emitted because a test intentionally triggers
a warning or error path:

```
W (...): <message>  — triggered by test #N, #M
```

Omit this section if no such log lines appear.

**Build configuration** — key compile-time constants that affect test behavior:
capacity limits, pool sizes, task counts, RTOS port, heap allocator variant.

**Resource overhead** — measure the module under test in isolation:

| Metric | Value | Measurement method |
|---|---|---|
| RAM — static | N bytes | `size` / map file, `.bss` + `.data` of the module |
| RAM — heap peak | N bytes | heap-before minus heap-after a representative operation sequence |
| Code size | N bytes | `size` / map file, `.text` + `.rodata` of the module |
| CPU per operation | N µs / N cycles | timestamp delta around the hot path; `esp_timer_get_time()` or cycle counter |

Rules:
- Report RAM heap peak for the worst-case scenario (maximum subscribers / instances active simultaneously)
- Report code size for the final release build (optimisation flags on, debug off)
- CPU measurement is optional when a cycle counter or high-resolution timer is unavailable; mark the cell `N/A` with the reason
- If the module imposes a documented resource budget, add a pass/fail column against that budget

### Formatting rules

- Result values: `Pass` or `Fail` — never `OK`, `FAIL`, `✓`, or `✗`
- No prose paragraphs inside group tables
- Uncovered path table must always appear, even if empty (`None`)
- Resource overhead table must always appear; use `N/A` for metrics that cannot be measured in the current environment
